Maximum number of printing characters that the pretty printer willingly puts on a line.
Standard Indentation Amount.
Maximum length of a `simple' list.
Is 'form as simple as form?
The name of the file containing the rules.  Looked up along the famous files path.
The looks to apply to Scheme keywords.
The looks to apply to formal arguments.
The looks to apply to the subject of a (define ...) form.
For singleton list, just print parens and recurse.
Defining an atom to be a simple expression gets special-case formatting.
A body-s is a series of expressions, each on their own line, indented a sia.
What gyrations we go through to handle: empty list, singleton, and regular case!
Fortunately, we can re-use that mess.
Within an (export ...), (define ...) forms are surrounded by blank lines; other forms are not.
Lots of special cases for (if ...).  In the simplest, the consequent and alternative are put in their own nested object.
The first rule could also be written more directly as expr (both ('cond ms object condSeries)), remembering that (a b c) is (a . (b . (c . ()))), but some people find that confusing.
The first rule is for named let, and groups the name and bindings into their own nest object.
The miser break will shift the whole body outward only if globally necessary.
Lots of re-use here.
To get particular formatting for the extension clauses:
We want to convert (quote x) into 'x, but not
(quote ;a comment here!
x)
into anything like 'x.  The reader macros should be undone even when printing data.
We have to count depth to know when we leave quasiquotation.
when depth reaches 0, pretty-print as expr
Undo reader macros, again.
We put the rules for procedure call at the end, 'cause otherwise they'd match most syntax that wants specific rules.  The classification of the head determines the condition of the first break (the earlier presence of a rule for singleton lists ensures that we have at least one argument).  Complex arguments (the last one is never considered complex) are separated from other args by always breaks; otherwise lookLeft breaks are used.
